From: apklayer
Subject: [PATCH] libhidl: the stand-in stays where the host has a hwservicemanager file (a Halium device) and the instance runs its own manager

apklayer patch (docs/PATCHES.md, docs/HIDL.md, docs/HALIUM.md). defaultServiceManager1_2 takes libhwbinder's isHidlSupported(), which is true when
/system/bin/hwservicemanager exists. The path layer serves a file the instance's Android root lacks from the host, and a Halium device
(Ubuntu Touch, Sailfish: /system is the Android image) has that file: with the instance's own manager running, every process got the
real client of that manager and never the stand-in, so listManifestByInterface, listByInterface and registerForNotifications for the listed
device HALs (camera provider, Codec2) were answered by the instance's manager with nothing (`Codec2Client: No Codec2 services declared`,
cameraserver without a HIDL provider). 0004's marker (the stack writes it when the instance has host HALs and runs the manager) now also
keeps the stand-in there. Rebuild: `m libhidlbase`.
Against system/libhidl 061055fc4dad (REVISION), after 0001-0004. Apply: patches/apply aosp <LineageOS tree>.
---
--- a/transport/ServiceManagement.cpp
+++ b/transport/ServiceManagement.cpp
@@ -425,7 +425,9 @@
             return nullptr;
         }
 
-        if (!isHidlSupported()) {
+        // apklayer patch (libhidl 0005): with the instance's own manager beside it the stand-in stays, as 0004 says, also where
+        // /system/bin/hwservicemanager exists (the path layer falls through to a Halium host's own): its list calls go to the host
+        if (!isHidlSupported() || details::localServiceManager() != nullptr) {
             // hwservicemanager is not available on this device.
             LOG(WARNING) << "hwservicemanager is not supported on the device.";
             gDefaultServiceManager = sp<NoHwServiceManager>::make();
